Settle a subagent's alerts when its parent is opened, and only buzz for a stuck one

Two changes to what raises a notification, both about the same thing: a parent and the
subagents it dispatched are one piece of work the reader attends to, not several.

Opening a session now settles its subagents' alerts as well as its own, and suppresses
theirs while it is in front. The read used to be a single id, so looking at a parent
left every child's alert in the shade for work that was already on screen.

And a subagent is worth a buzz only when it is *stuck*. An approval or a question stalls
a child outright and the parent's screen cannot show it — the header counts subagents
that are running, and a child waiting for a human is running as far as that count is
concerned — which is precisely the failure this layer exists for. A child's error or a
bell in its shell is not that: the parent's turn carries on, the transcript shows what
happened, and a notification per child turns one dispatch into a stream of buzzes for
work nobody asked to supervise directly. So `alert` takes `blocking`, and only the two
waterfalls pass it.

`subagentTreeIds` is breadth-first and cycle-safe, with five tests. 342 green.